gging.LogRecord copy\n```\n\nAlternative approach: compute full message = record.getMessage(); truncated = self._truncate(full). Then temporarily set record.msg = truncated, record.args = None, then call super().format(record), then restore in finally. That mutates but restores — risky with threads though. Better to shallow-copy: \n\n```python\nimport copy\nrecord = copy.copy(record)\nrecord.msg = truncated\nrecord.args = None\nrecord.message = truncated  # super().format sets record.message\n```\n\nNote `logging.Formatter.format` sets record.message = record.getMessage(). Copy approach is clean and doesn't touch original.\n\nBut wait: prefix computation in existing format uses `record.message` after super().format — that'll be the truncated message, fine.\n\nAlso exception info is preserved in copy.\n\nNow truncation calc: budget = max_message_chars; marker = \"...<omitted>...\" len 15. remaining = budget - 15. head = (remaining + 1)//2, tail = remaining - head. For budget 32: remaining=17, head=9, tail=8. ✓\n\nIf budget <= len(marker)... budget>=32 so fine.\n\nNow environ: add `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none()`? But must be >=32 and error message containing variable name. int_or_none gives int(v) if not None — empty string → ValueError from int(\"\") which is \"invalid literal for int() with base 10: ''\" — the descriptor wraps with name prefix so message contains variable name. But must validate >=32. Add a factory method to EnvVar:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_min(min_value: int) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < min_value:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{value}, must be >= {min_value}\")\n            return value\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\n\nHmm but the test might check error message contains variable name — the descriptor wraps. Careful: the `__get__` error